CONVERSATION:
&quot;Hello,&quot; he said.
what is air?
&quot;Hi,&quot; she said.
&quot;Air is essentially a bunch of chemical compounds,&quot; he replied.
&quot;What are the typical odds of meeting a female capable of typing with perfect sentence structure?&quot; he returned a riddle of his own.
She snorted, &quot;Mostly likely a very low chance, but I may be the one to have it.&quot;
&quot;Very low, indeed; intelligence is a trait seemingly rarer than gold in our sick, sad world.&quot; He figured it best to keep the lid on his pessimist for at least a while longer. &quot;So, what&apos;s the time where you&apos;re at?&quot; he inquired.
&quot;It is 11 minutes until midnight and I agree, intelligence is very rare to stumble upon now. What time is it where you are, plus why are speaking like this?&quot; Tiltinh her head a little to the right out of old habit.
&quot;It&apos;s roughly an hour later here on the east coast. Erm, I dunno, third-person is interesting because it subconsciously lowers inhibition a few degrees, but that&apos;s merely speculation from an armchair psychologist. It&apos;s mostly so that I can squeeze in &apos;he said&apos; to allow people looking for females to disconnect so that I can move on to more interesting discussions. We can drop it any time, if you&apos;d like, but I won&apos;t be the one to commit such blasphemy.&quot; He noted the adorable gesture and momentarily lost the ability to produce a response. Eventually, though: &quot;Up late on a Thursday, then? Surely there&apos;s a mildly interesting story behind the situation, no?&quot; he asked, offering a warm smile to combat the undoubted creepiness he suspected he might be projecting.
&quot;Well third person usually makes me drop back into my roleplaying mindset is all. Yay psychologist are usually fun to talk to hah, never know what they discern just from a typing style.&quot; Smiling really quickly, giggling a little. &quot;Yup, I wasn&apos;t sleepy and I&apos;m always wanting a conversation, surprisingly I found one, thanks to you. Why are you up late though?&quot; Looking at him curiously when she returned his question for him to answer.
&quot;A former role-player! What a truly joyous occasion. It&apos;s rather interesting to note how often proper typists are writers of some sort. Would you mind if I asked what sort of role-playing you did?&quot; He took a moment to gather his thoughts, stunned by just how many endearing qualities this one person engendered. &quot;A psychologist is a dangerous beast, though, for the very reasons you hint at; I do indeed think that quite a bit can be gleaned merely from the way a person carries themselves in an electronic conversation, but that&apos;s boring science stuff that we&apos;ll leave alone for now.&quot; He stopped short, figuring almost certainly that he was talking too much, but still relieved by the fact that she was somehow still here. &quot;One in the morning is incredibly early for me, particularly since I&apos;ve only been awake about a day.&quot; He paused, strongly considering keeping the next bit secret before figuring she was somebody who&apos;d at least mildly understand it: &quot;I&apos;m a terrible insomniac whose brain doesn&apos;t allow him to sleep like a normal person; rather, I usually stay awake two and three days at a time so that falling asleep is eventually much easier.&quot; He offered a sheepish smile to accompany the revelation that would almost certainly make her consider him insane; the contrast was positively silly.
&quot;Mostly a mishmash of things, mostly just casual though. Never was any good at the others types.&quot; Shurgging, thinking for a moment then saying, &quot;Yes it is actually, I am very good at changing the style of which I type if I ever need to trick someone that I am not myslef but mostly for fun.&quot; Laughing under her breath, knowing that might be kind of scary-creepy. &quot;Man staying awake that long would be incredly annoying to me. Even though I love being up at night I just as equally love sleeping since I have school ever morning; which is terrible for I hate mornings something bad. Although as long as your healthy sleep however oftn your body makes ya.&quot; Covering her mouth as she yawned because she was actually tired but too interested now.
Alas, I may be a heathen for it, but delays between our messages is no good if you&apos;re sleepy.
