CONVERSATION:
Any way to do something like transform: matrix(a, b, c, d);  but where the vals, are the coords for the corners?  Not to seem math uninclined, but.. it seems much more obvio..er.. simple. :)
Or does someone more mathematically inclined see a simple way to convert coords-to-matrix?  I have to be honest, I don't quite grasp the geometry / math of the matrix transform.
what are you trying to do.
a transform matrix does rotation and translation in one step. if you just want to translate, there's the translate(x y) option
0de37da1d654c10aed33d7b7347b8e3b: I was just thinking of a (possibly better, definitely simpler) way of defining the transform.  requiring the UA to handle calculating the angles, and simply defining the points for the corners.
but what do you mean with &quot;the corners&quot;?
0de37da1d654c10aed33d7b7347b8e3b: on the basis of a square.
that still leaves me puzzled as to what kind of transform you are thinking about
0de37da1d654c10aed33d7b7347b8e3b: perhaps I am mis-perceiving how the actual rendering model / transformation is handled.
0de37da1d654c10aed33d7b7347b8e3b: assume that you have a square
possibly, but I still have no idea what you actually want to *do* =)
okay
0de37da1d654c10aed33d7b7347b8e3b: rather than defining the mathematical matrix, you define the points for the four corners.
no, step back, because I don't know what you think &quot;the matrix&quot; does.
I have a square, what is it you want to do to this square?
0de37da1d654c10aed33d7b7347b8e3b: in my example you specify the coordinates, (probably relative to their initial positions).
