and is the only behaviour i've found in the four browsers i tested that is even remotely sane.
anyway, as i noted in my e-mail reply, just use the text node's own buffer as the buffer
69b0d3dfe919a6b860a9fac82de52a7e: you have an interesting notion of sane
and then you get IE's behaviour for free
really? you don't think having things come out in the order they were written is sane?
69b0d3dfe919a6b860a9fac82de52a7e: nope, then you get the Firefox behavior
you only get firefox's behaviour if you don't check to make sure the text node is still the last child each time a script runs
(which is a trivial pointer test)
69b0d3dfe919a6b860a9fac82de52a7e: my independent implementation matched webkit and opera, and I like to think I'm sane
i'm not saying the implementor isn't sane
i'm saying that having nodes come out in an order different than the order they went in is unintuitive
depends on whether your intuition is buffered :-)
