e6f7f95dae81fe5bc3080cb1c5628d1e, the new grammar still doesn't solve the problem...
It's also not clear to me how you keep the semantics in the language this way... as you're essential..." | raw="
CONVERSATION:
e6f7f95dae81fe5bc3080cb1c5628d1e, the new grammar still doesn't solve the problem...
It's also not clear to me how you keep the semantics in the language this way... as you're essentially putting them in some subset of ECMAScript too...
hsivonen, maybe you should post about versioning on your blog too?
reads http://lists.w3.org/Archives/Public/public-html/2007JanMar/0433
Good evening Anne! Can you explain why the grammar + semantic constraints don't solve the problem.  This shouldn't be rocket science as excel and XForms have no problems with such expressions.
what if the function is not side effect free?
(as I understand it XForms doesn't allow authors to use functions that have side effects)
(I'm not familiar enough with Excel scripting languages.)
so I am looking to describe what the UA reasonably needs to do, but we can't easily stop people who insist on shooting themselves in the foot when using scripting languages.
the behavior would need to be defined exactly otherwise it's not really feasible
If you write a function, it is up to you to avoid doing something silly. That has always been the case for scripting.
I think that was already mentioned in the previous thread we had about this
Surely you are not telling me that Opera statically analyses all scripts set as event handlers?
event handlers have a clear defined processing model
huh!
anyway, this is just reiterating the same thread as before
I don't see much value in that
That thread wasn't satisfactory from a technical view point nor from consideration of authors who don't know and don't want to learn scripting.
The market will fix this if HTML isn't a good authoring language.
It's still not clear to me why authors would not learn HTML but would learn you expression language and why they can't use some simplified expression language that's mapped to ECMAScript by some tool (as I assume Google Docs does it if they offer such functionality).
It you want to create a spreadsheet you just need to understand how to write simple formulae, and how to set cell types, e.g. to dates.
That is really nice for non-technical types.
A good editor would allow them to do the same for web base apps without having to learn html or css.
Not everyone is a programmer.
Well yes, I agree with that.
s/you expression/your expression/
Would you instead accept a subset of XPath?
This could be reversably mapped to something like JavaScript expressions for the author, to address ease of use.
As said above, it's not clear to me what's wrong with event handlers. Especially since the people who are building this stuff don't have much interest in it and not much people see obvious use cases for it.
What's wrong with event handler is that they require you to be a programmer to understand what the script does and how to write an event handler.
That's not very considerate to the majority of the population.
The majority of the population would use an editor, right?
doesn't see why the editor can't abstract away the &quot;complexity&quot;
The HTML WG is a self selecting bunch of geeks - I don't see anyway around that, but we do have a duty of care to other people.
The majority of the population won't get XPath or your subset of ECMAScript with side effect free functions either.
How would the editor be able to understand the semantics of code?
We all agree the impracticality of machine reasoning over turing complete code.
presumably editors would figure that out amongst themselves
how, are they AI complete?
The only way they could is for them to save a declarative representation somewhere.
if they can write it they can prolly read it too
That pretty much eliminates interoperability doesn't it?
&quot;presumably editors would figure that out amongst themselves&quot;
You would only be able to use the editor the document was created with - sounds like a great lockin for a proprietary format,
I thought we all agreed on the need for open standards and interoperability
anyway, it would make more sense to me if you actually said this on the mailing list instead of trying to abstract around it with some proposal everybody objects against
then again, I'm not sure many people were convinced of the need for this...
yes, I will try to capture this in my email reply to your response.
If the HTML WG only addresses the needs for people who are programmers, HTML will become increasingly irrelevant except as a delivery format.
The market will see to that.
I haven't seen that happening so far
so far HTML is pretty much the only delivery format on the web...
Open Office is an example that is picking up, especially in government circles.
as a replacement for Word documents, maybe
Companies producing content for mobile devices are increasingly using more declarative formats than HTML - my company is thriving"")
